# download-probe -- THE DEVICE-DOWNLOAD ALLOWLIST
#
# `download-probe` is the ONE binary in this repository that calls DownloadProvider.Download, and a
# download is DEVICE-LEVEL: it transfers the whole PLC software and stops the CPU. This file names
# the projects it may open. Everything else is refused with exit 3, BEFORE Portal is contacted.
#
# *** ALLOWLIST, NEVER A DENYLIST (ADR-0011 requirement 1). *** As of 2026-08-13 this engineering PC
# carries about NINETEEN real production .ap20 projects in sibling folders of the scratch ones, so "the
# project nobody thought to list" is not hypothetical here. A denylist would have to be complete, and
# it would stop being complete the next time a job folder arrived.
#
# *** WHY THIS SUPERSEDED A FILE-NAME SUFFIX (2026-08-13) -- AND THE SUFFIX WAS NOT ARBITRARY. ***
# The guard used to accept any project whose file name ended " scratch.ap20". That convention had a
# real origin: the scratch COPIES of live jobs on this machine are named that way, so the suffix was
# fitted to the projects the tool was actually being pointed at, and it deliberately avoided naming
# any of their paths in the repository (see the data-boundary note below -- that constraint is real
# and this file still honours it). It is superseded because it was wrong in both DIRECTIONS, not
# because it was careless:
#
#   * IT BLOCKED THE LEGITIMATE CASE. The S6 sandbox project is GenProject1.ap20 and the other repo
#     scratch project is SampleProject.ap20; neither ends in the suffix, so neither could ever be the
#     target of a download however deliberately somebody chose it. Renaming a live Openness-managed
#     project folder was judged not worth the risk for a naming-only change (CLAUDE.md "Codename
#     note"), so this was not fixable by renaming.
#   * IT DID NOT STOP THE DANGEROUS CASE. A file-name suffix is a CONVENTION, and anything can be
#     renamed into one. A guard that a private engineering project could satisfy by rename is not
#     protecting the nineteen sitting beside the scratch ones.
#
# *** THERE IS NO OVERRIDE. *** No flag, no environment variable, no argument that names a different
# allowlist. Adding a project here is a decision, made once, by the owner, with the restore point
# named -- it is not something an agent does to get a run through.
#
# FORMAT
#   One project per line. Blank lines and lines starting with # are ignored.
#   Each entry is the path to the project FILE (.apNN), in one of two forms:
#     repo:<path>   resolved against the root of the repository this binary was built in. Portable
#                   across checkouts and git worktrees, which is why the repo projects use it.
#     <absolute>    a fully-qualified path, e.g. D:\Scratch\Thing\Thing.ap20
#   A bare relative path is REFUSED rather than guessed at.
#
# An entry that does not exist on this machine simply never matches. That fails CLOSED, which is the
# safe direction, so it is not itself an error.
#
# *** DO NOT WRITE A ROOTED PATH AFTER "repo:". MEASURED 2026-08-14, AND THE TWO FENCES THAT READ
# THIS FORMAT DISAGREE ABOUT WHAT IT MEANS. *** The prefix is resolved by joining the entry onto the
# repository root, and a join DISCARDS the root when the second part is already rooted:
#
#     entry                              download-probe (C#)          confirm-roundtrip.ps1 (PS 5.1)
#     ---------------------------------  ---------------------------  ------------------------------
#     repo:C:\Somewhere\Job.ap20         C:\Somewhere\Job.ap20        C:\<repo>\C:\Somewhere\Job.ap20
#                                        *** ESCAPES THE REPO ***     (nonsense path, never matches)
#     repo:\GenProject1\GenProject1.ap20 C:\GenProject1\...           C:\<repo>\GenProject1\...
#                                        (the DRIVE ROOT)             (what you meant)
#
# The second row is the one to fear: a LEADING BACKSLASH is the likeliest typo in this format, and
# it silently means two different places to the two fences. Both happen to fail closed TODAY because
# no project sits at either location -- that is luck, not design, and it is why this is written down
# rather than relied on. The rule: after "repo:", write a path RELATIVE to the repository root and
# nothing else. If you want an absolute path, use the absolute form -- it is supported above, it is
# unambiguous, and both fences agree about it.
#
# The SEMANTICS ARE DELIBERATELY UNCHANGED (2026-08-14). Redefining what an existing allowlist entry
# means is an owner decision with a review behind it, not something an agent does at four in the
# morning while hunting for holes. Current behaviour is pinned by a test so a future change to it is
# a decision rather than a drift.
#
# *** A PROJECT WHOSE PATH MAY NOT BE COMMITTED DOES NOT GO HERE -- NOT EVEN AS AN EXAMPLE. *** A
# copy of a live engineering job may not have its path, folder name or file name written into this
# repository in any form (CLAUDE.md, "Live runs": use anything, commit nothing; docs/13). This file
# is TRACKED, so such a path goes in the machine-local allowlist instead, at the fixed path
#     %ProgramData%\Ladder-AI\download-probe.allowlist
# which download-probe reads as well as this one. Same format, same weight, same absence of any
# override.
#
# WHY OUTSIDE THE REPOSITORY RATHER THAN A GITIGNORED COMPANION BESIDE THIS FILE. A gitignored file
# inside the repo is protected by a PATTERN, and a pattern protects the file somebody thought of. On
# 2026-08-13 an agent's `.claude/settings.local.json.bak` sat untracked but UNIGNORED, one `git add
# -A` from committing a site path -- the tracked file was protected and the thing beside it was
# not. A path outside the working tree cannot be committed by any accident of ignore rules, by any
# `git add -f`, and by any tool that walks the repo. It is not a weaker fence: it is the same file
# with the same rules, in the one place where a mistake cannot publish it.

# *** root: ENTRIES, ADDED 2026-08-28 ON THE OWNER'S INSTRUCTION. *** A "root:" entry names a
# DIRECTORY and permits every .apNN at or under it, so a new project in an area somebody has already
# named needs no edit to this file. The owner's ask was to stop the fence demanding a per-project
# decision for ordinary work; this is that, without turning the fence into a wildcard.
#
# WHAT DID NOT CHANGE, DELIBERATELY. An entry WITHOUT the prefix still means exactly the one file it
# always meant -- this file's own rule above is that redefining an existing entry's meaning is an
# owner decision with a review behind it, and widening a fence is not the moment to also change what
# its existing lines say. Both forms are pinned by tests, including that a root which is only a NAME
# PREFIX of a directory ("...\Sand" against "...\Sandbox") does NOT match: on a machine whose whole
# risk is private projects in SIBLING folders, the separator is the entire check.
#
# THESE TWO ROOTS COVER THE REPOSITORY'S OWN SCRATCH PROJECTS. They are deliberately NOT a root over
# the repository, because "Live Runs/" sits inside it and holds private engineering projects -- including
# the REAL JOB9004 project, not just its scratch copies. A root there would permit downloading a
# site's live program to the controller that project points at, which is the one thing this
# fence exists to prevent.
root:repo:GenProject1
root:repo:Test Enviroment

# The S6 sandbox / validation project. Called test-project001 everywhere in docs and IR; the live
# Portal folder on disk keeps its original name (CLAUDE.md, "Codename note").
repo:GenProject1/GenProject1.ap20

# The test-environment project, added 2026-08-14 on the owner's explicit decision in session.
# THE RESTORE POINT, as this file's header requires one to be named: a full 45-object
# `export-all --tagtables` baseline, plus the delete-all -> `import-all` restore sequence, both
# PROVEN against this project the same day (36 blocks deleted, 45 restored in 3 fixpoint passes,
# 45/45 Normalizer-equivalent to the baseline by `converter compare`).
repo:Test Enviroment/TestEnviroment/TestEnviroment.ap20
